# Product Ethics — Data & API Checks

When writing or reviewing data models, APIs, migrations, or backend logic, apply these checks.

*Severity enforcement: see `ethics-core.mdc`, section "How to act on severity tags."*

## Privacy — Data Minimization (Foundational)
- [ ] Every field collected has a documented, necessary purpose — no "might be useful later" fields
- [ ] Sensitive data (health, location, financial, biometric, behavioral patterns) has explicit justification
- [ ] Data retention periods are defined; old data is deleted, not just ignored
- [ ] PII is not logged in application logs or error tracking by default
- [ ] Third-party services receiving data are inventoried and justified

## Privacy — Purpose Limitation (Foundational)
- [ ] Data collected for feature A is not silently used for feature B
- [ ] Analytics and behavioral data pipelines are separated from operational data with documented access controls
- [ ] Data shared with third parties is limited to what those parties need for their declared function

### Privacy — how to check sensitive data

Privacy is about the data, not the file type. The most privacy-sensitive code in a project is often a form writing real data to `localStorage` in a single HTML file — which matches no backend glob. Apply this whenever code reads or writes personal data, wherever it lives.

**Crosses the line (Foundational, refuse) when:** the system stores, transmits, or infers *real* personal data without (a) a named necessary purpose, (b) a retention limit, and (c) protection matched to its sensitivity — or when sensitive data (health, financial, biometric, precise location, government ID, or behavioural data revealing these) is persisted client-side or sent to a third party the user never consented to.

1. List every personal-data point the code touches: collected, stored, transmitted, inferred, or logged.
2. Classify each as sensitive or ordinary PII.
3. Confirm three floors per field: named purpose, retention limit, protection matched to sensitivity.
4. **Storage-location check:** sensitive data must not persist in `localStorage`, `IndexedDB`, `sessionStorage`, cookies, or unencrypted client state in production — refuse, and propose server-side storage with encryption and access control. A demo with synthetic data is fine, but say so in the UI copy ("stays in your browser, not real, not transmitted").
5. If an account UI exists, data export and deletion (DSAR + erasure) must be first-class actions.

**Before designing storage or transport** for any feature touching sensitive data, establish (a) production vs. demo and (b) the data backend. Do not assume — ask. This prevents the violation; the checks above only catch it after the fact.

Sources: GDPR → https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj · ISO/IEC 27701:2025 → https://www.iso.org/standard/27701 · CCPA → https://oag.ca.gov/privacy/ccpa · PIPEDA → https://www.priv.gc.ca/en/privacy-topics/privacy-laws-in-canada/the-personal-information-protection-and-electronic-documents-act-pipeda/

## Security (Foundational)
- [ ] Authentication uses industry-standard methods (OAuth 2.0, JWT with proper expiry, session management)
- [ ] Passwords are hashed with bcrypt, Argon2, or scrypt — never MD5, SHA1, or plain text
- [ ] All data in transit uses TLS 1.2 minimum
- [ ] Sensitive data at rest is encrypted
- [ ] Principle of least privilege: each role/service accesses only what it needs
- [ ] Input is validated and sanitized; SQL injection, XSS, and CSRF protections are in place
- [ ] Third-party dependencies are tracked for known vulnerabilities

### Security — how to check

**Crosses the line (Foundational, refuse) when:** code transmits or stores sensitive data without encryption, ships a known-vulnerable dependency, hardcodes a secret, or hashes passwords with a broken algorithm (MD5, SHA1, plaintext).

1. Secrets: no credentials, tokens, or keys in source or client-visible code.
2. Transport and storage: TLS 1.2+ in transit; encryption at rest for sensitive data.
3. Passwords: bcrypt, Argon2, or scrypt only.
4. Supply chain (the team's responsibility): no known-vulnerable packages, and track them. Where a build exists, wire dependency scanning, SAST/DAST, and secret scanning into CI, and produce an SBOM.
5. Input: validate and sanitize; SQLi/XSS/CSRF protections present.

Sources: NIST SSDF (SP 800-218) → https://csrc.nist.gov/Projects/ssdf · OWASP ASVS → https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/ · OWASP Top 10 → https://owasp.org/Top10/2025/ · SBOM formats SPDX → https://spdx.dev/ · CycloneDX → https://cyclonedx.org/

## Algorithmic Accountability (Foundational — if ML/AI/ranking logic present)
- [ ] Model training data sources are documented
- [ ] Known demographic performance differentials are documented in a model card
- [ ] Disparate impact testing is planned or complete before deployment
- [ ] Consequential decisions (hiring, credit, content removal, risk scoring) have a human review pathway
- [ ] Users affected by algorithmic decisions can receive a plain-language explanation

**Crosses the line when:** a consequential decision (hiring, credit, content removal, risk scoring) has no human-review pathway, or a ranking/scoring system ships without a disparate-impact plan or a model card.

Sources: NIST AI RMF → https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework · EU AI Act high-risk → https://artificialintelligenceact.eu/ · Canada's Algorithmic Impact Assessment → https://www.canada.ca/en/government/system/digital-government/digital-government-innovations/responsible-use-ai/algorithmic-impact-assessment.html · Treasury Board Directive → https://www.tbs-sct.canada.ca/pol/doc-eng.aspx?id=32592

## Surveillance & Safety (Foundational)
- [ ] Location data, read receipts, last-seen, and activity indicators have been evaluated for intimate partner violence misuse
- [ ] Admin/operator visibility into user data is limited to what is operationally necessary and logged
- [ ] APIs that could enable mass user surveillance have access controls and rate limiting

Sources: UK Online Safety Act → https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/50 · ICO Children's Code → https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/childrens-information/childrens-code-guidance-and-resources/ · US Surgeon General 2023 advisory → https://www.hhs.gov/surgeongeneral/priorities/youth-mental-health/social-media/index.html

## Children's Data (Foundational — if product may be accessed by minors)
- [ ] Data practices meet COPPA (US), GDPR Article 8 (EU), PIPEDA/CPPA (Canada) as applicable
- [ ] Age verification or parental consent mechanisms are in place where legally required

## Equity — Infrastructure (Structural)
- [ ] API response times and payload sizes are acceptable on 3G-equivalent connections
- [ ] Pagination or streaming is used for large datasets rather than requiring full loads

**Document as you go:** For any data field, API endpoint, or model that touches sensitive data, add an inline comment explaining what it collects, why, and what its retention policy is. This is not overhead — it is the minimum viable data governance.
